{
 "id": "present-subjunctive",
 "title": "Present Subjunctive",
 "course": "Verbs · Subjunctive",
 "subtitle": "Active and passive: all four conjugations plus 3rd -iō",
 "intro": "The present subjunctive changes the <b>vowel</b> before the personal ending:\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>1st (amō):</b> ā → <b>ē</b>: am<b>e</b>m, am<b>ē</b>s, am<b>e</b>t</li>\n      <li><b>2nd (videō):</b> ē → <b>ea</b>: vid<b>ea</b>m, vid<b>eā</b>s, vid<b>ea</b>t</li>\n      <li><b>3rd (mittō):</b> e/i → <b>a</b>: mitt<b>a</b>m, mitt<b>ā</b>s, mitt<b>a</b>t</li>\n      <li><b>3rd -iō (capiō):</b> i → <b>ia</b>: cap<b>ia</b>m, cap<b>iā</b>s, cap<b>ia</b>t</li>\n      <li><b>4th (audiō):</b> ī → <b>ia</b>: aud<b>ia</b>m, aud<b>iā</b>s, aud<b>ia</b>t</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\">Mnemonic: “w<b>e</b> h<b>ea</b>r <b>a</b> l<b>ia</b>r fr<b>ia</b>r”: one word for each conjugation, in order (<b>e</b>, <b>ea</b>, <b>a</b>, <b>ia</b>, <b>ia</b>).</p>\n    <p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\">The endings are the regular personal endings, but the 1st singular active is <b>-m</b>, not -ō:\n    active <span class=\"end\">-m, -s, -t, -mus, -tis, -nt</span>; passive <span class=\"end\">-r, -ris, -tur, -mur, -minī, -ntur</span>.</p>\n    <p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\"><b>Watch out:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>mittam, capiam, audiam</b> (and passive mittar, capiar, audiar) are identical to the future indicative. Context decides.</li>\n      <li>amēs and amet look like 2nd-conjugation present indicative (vidēs, videt), and mittās and mittat are not 1st conjugation. Always check the verb's conjugation first.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\"><b>Translation:</b> the present subjunctive is translated with <b>“may”</b> (amet, “he may love”; amētur, “he may be loved”). The imperfect subjunctive will use “might.”</p>",
 "paradigmNote": "Active: <span class=\"end\">-m, -s, -t, -mus, -tis, -nt</span><br>Passive: <span class=\"end\">-r, -ris, -tur, -mur, -minī, -ntur</span>",
